# Every text file checks out with LF, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference - it is what makes the byte-stability ratchet mean
# anything, and here it is load-bearing twice over.
#
# A parse bundle embeds the program's source VERBATIM, along with the sha256 that pins it
# (`parse_bundle.py`). Left to git's autocrlf, a Windows checkout carries `\r\n` into that
# text and into that hash, so `goldens/parse.sha256` describes the filesystem the bundle
# was built on rather than the member - and a bundle written on one box would not match
# the same source read on another.
#
# A member resolved from the local search path is also read as BYTES, and its length goes
# into the retrieval reports the dependent packages hash (`bytes: 128`).
#
# COBOL is fixed-format source besides, where a byte count is a fact about the member
# rather than an artifact of the filesystem it landed on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files whose exact bytes are the contract.
*.py      text eol=lf
*.cbl     text eol=lf
*.cpy     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
